---
/* Ported from docs/kitchen-sink/review.html (CCWEB2-317 phase 3).
 *
 * Shell extraction. Mounting pattern and why it is not an island: button.astro.

 * The head <style> is story-only and load-bearing twice over: the card is
 * transparent with a 32px backdrop blur, so it can only be judged over the real
 * harvested plate, and the grid story needs one continuous ground for two
 * cards. `is:inline` keeps its selectors unscoped; its @media braces sit inside
 * a <style> and are left alone. The ${className} in the findings prose is not —
 * that one is guarded, being template text about a template. */
import DocPage from '../../layouts/DocPage.astro';
---
<DocPage
  title="Review — CODECAVE storybook"
  nav="kitchen-sink"
  stylesheets={['../storybook/tw-bridge.css','storybook.css']}
  importmap
>
<style slot="head" is:inline>
  /* Story-only. The testimonial card is transparent with a 32px backdrop blur,
     so it only reads correctly over the decorative art it is designed to sit on.
     This canvas supplies that ground from the real harvested SVG.

     testimonials-background.svg, specifically — the plate named for this
     section. The page used to mount the cards on partnership-background.svg,
     which is a different section's art (concentric circles, not the diagonal
     line field), so the blur was being judged against a ground the component
     never meets. Each plate carries its own #050505 rect by design
     (DESIGN.md sect. "Imagery"), which is why no page colour shows through. */
  .on-art { background: var(--color-surface-primary) url("../imagery/testimonials-background.svg") center/cover no-repeat; }

  /* The grid story. Cards are laid two-up over ONE continuous plate, because
     that is the only arrangement in which the transparency does any work: you
     can see the art run behind a card and out the other side, and you can see
     where one card's blur stops and its neighbour's begins. The 16px alley is
     not this stylesheet's — it is the component's own `mx-1 lg:mx-2` doubled.
     Nothing here sets a card width; the grid track does, as a section wrapper
     would. */
  .rev-grid { display: grid; gap: 0; grid-template-columns: 1fr; padding: 2.5rem 0; }
  @media (min-width: 60rem) { .rev-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
</style>
<div class="sb">
  <a class="sb-back" href="index.html">&larr; All components</a>

  <header class="sb-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Content · 8 of 13</p>
    <h1>Review</h1>
    <p>The attributed testimonial. It has no background of its own — only a 32px
       <code>backdrop-filter</code> — so it is the one component that cannot be
       judged on a plain surface.</p>
    <div class="sb-tags">
      <span class="sb-tag is-vue">Vue island</span>
      <span class="sb-tag">src/components/common/Review.vue</span>
      <span class="sb-tag">rendered live from compiled/Review.js</span>
      <span class="sb-tag">Strapi</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="sb-section">
    <h2>Props</h2>
    <table class="sb-props">
      <thead><tr><th>Prop</th><th>Type</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>item <span class="sb-req">*</span></td><td><code>Testimonial</code></td><td>Reads <code>photo.url</code>, <code>photo.name</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>position</code>, <code>review</code>, <code>linkedinurl</code>, <code>verification</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>className</td><td><code>string</code></td><td>Interpolated with no fallback — see findings.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="sb-section">
    <h2>Stories</h2>
    <p>Shown over <code>testimonials-background.svg</code>, the plate named for
       the section this card ships in. The blur is what separates the card from
       the art behind it — there is nothing else doing that job.</p>
    <div class="sb-stories is-wide">

      <div class="sb-story">
        <div class="sb-canvas is-block on-art">
          <div id="story-rev1"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="sb-story-body">
          <span class="sb-story-name">Full</span>
          <span class="sb-story-args">photo + linkedinurl + verification</span>
          <span class="sb-story-note">Verification row is <code>--color-action</code> — see findings.
            The LinkedIn icon renders because <code>linkedinurl='#!'</code> passes the
            <code>length&nbsp;&gt;&nbsp;1</code> guard (also see findings).</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sb-story">
        <div class="sb-canvas is-block on-art">
          <div id="story-rev2"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="sb-story-body">
          <span class="sb-story-name">Minimal</span>
          <span class="sb-story-args">no photo · no linkedinurl · no verification</span>
          <span class="sb-story-note">Photo is hidden by a literal filename check:
            <code>v-if="item.photo.name !== 'no-image.svg'"</code>. This story also
            omits <code>className</code> — inspect the card's class attribute and the
            literal string <code>undefined</code> is in it (see findings).</span>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sb-section">
    <h2>In the grid</h2>
    <p>One card on a canvas shows the blur; it does not show what the blur is
       <em>for</em>. Laid two-up over a single continuous plate, the art runs
       behind each card and out the other side, the alleys between them stay
       unblurred, and the falloff in the plate's opacity ladder puts every card
       on a slightly different ground — which is the case the component has to
       survive and the one a flat swatch cannot pose.</p>
    <div class="sb-stories is-single">
      <div class="sb-story">
        <div class="sb-canvas is-block on-art">
          <div class="rev-grid" id="story-grid"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="sb-story-body">
          <span class="sb-story-name">Four testimonials, two-up</span>
          <span class="sb-story-args">mixed: with and without photo, LinkedIn and verification</span>
          <span class="sb-story-note">The 16px alley is the component's own
            <code>mx-1&nbsp;lg:mx-2</code>, doubled where two cards meet — no wrapper
            supplies it. The section wrapper that positions this grid on the live
            site is an Astro page, not a harvested SFC, so its widths and vertical
            rhythm are <strong>not</strong> reproduced here; only the card is.
            The third card's review carries hard newlines to exercise
            <code>whitespace-pre-wrap</code>.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sb-section">
    <h2>Findings</h2>

    <p class="sb-note is-warn"><strong>A sentinel filename controls rendering.</strong>
      The avatar is gated on <code>item.photo.name !== 'no-image.svg'</code>. The
      contract between CMS and component is a magic string: rename the placeholder
      asset in Strapi and every testimonial sprouts a broken avatar. There is also
      no guard on <code>photo</code> itself, so a testimonial with no photo
      relation throws on <code>photo.name</code> rather than falling back.</p>

    <p class="sb-note is-warn"><strong><code>linkedinurl.length > 1</code> is a truthiness test in disguise.</strong>
      It guards against the empty string and a single character, not against an
      invalid URL. Any one-character-plus value renders a live external link.
      The same expression throws if <code>linkedinurl</code> is null rather than
      empty.</p>

    <p class="sb-note is-warn"><strong>The verification row fails contrast.</strong>
      <code>text-action</code> on the site's #0A0A0B ground is 2.94:1, below the
      4.5:1 floor for text this size (it was 3.03:1 against the pre-redesign
      #050505 — the rebuild nudged it further down). It is also the trust signal on a
      testimonial, so it is load-bearing copy. <code>--color-hovered</code>
      (≈8.4:1) is the in-palette fix. Same shape as <code>DESIGN.md</code> §10.5.</p>

    <p class="sb-note"><strong><code>className</code> has no fallback.</strong>
      The template interpolates <code>${'{'}className{'}'}</code> directly, unlike
      ArticlePreview's <code>${'{'}className ?? ''{'}'}</code>. Omitting the prop puts the
      literal string <code>undefined</code> into the class list. Harmless as a
      class name, but it ships in the markup.</p>

    <p class="sb-note"><strong>Judge it on art, never on flat color.</strong>
      With no background of its own, previewing this card on <code>#0A0A0B</code>
      makes the blur invisible and the design look unfinished. That is a preview
      error, not a component defect.</p>
  </section>

</div>

<script is:inline type="module">
  import { createApp } from 'vue';
  import Review from '../storybook/compiled/Review.js';
  import { usePlaceholders } from '../storybook/placeholders.js';

  createApp(Review, {
    item: {
      photo: { name: 'marta-feld.png', url: '/uploads/marta-feld.png' },
      name: 'Marta Feld',
      position: 'CTO, Northwind Logistics',
      review: 'They scoped the smallest thing that would prove the integration worked, shipped it in three weeks, and only then talked about the rest. The estimate held.',
      linkedinurl: '#!',
      verification: 'Verified on Clutch',
    },
    className: '',
  }).mount('#story-rev1');

  createApp(Review, {
    item: {
      photo: { name: 'no-image.svg', url: '' },
      name: 'Anonymous',
      position: 'Head of Engineering',
      review: 'Short and unattributed, to show the card with every optional branch off.',
      linkedinurl: '',
      verification: '',
    },
    // className omitted on purpose — the findings show what that does.
  }).mount('#story-rev2');

  /* ---- the grid story ---------------------------------------------------
   * Four cards over one plate. Each createApp().mount() takes over the
   * element it is given, so the grid gets four children to mount into rather
   * than one — the .rev-grid track sizes them, exactly as a section wrapper
   * would; the component sets no width of its own.
   *
   * The mix is deliberate: two cards with photos and two without, so the
   * `photo.name !== 'no-image.svg'` branch is visible side by side, and the
   * verification row appears on some cards and not others — which is how the
   * section actually looks, not how a matrix of one variant looks. */
  const GRID = [
    {
      photo: { name: 'a.png', url: '/uploads/a.png' },
      name: 'Marta Feld', position: 'CTO, Northwind Logistics, Germany',
      review: 'They scoped the smallest thing that would prove the integration worked, shipped it in three weeks, and only then talked about the rest. The estimate held.',
      linkedinurl: '#!', verification: 'Clutch Verified',
    },
    {
      photo: { name: 'no-image.svg', url: '' },
      name: 'Dario Benn', position: 'Head of Platform, Kestrel Freight',
      review: 'Handover was the part I expected to go badly. The documentation was written as the work happened rather than after it, so there was nothing to reconstruct.',
      linkedinurl: '#!', verification: '',
    },
    {
      photo: { name: 'c.png', url: '/uploads/c.png' },
      name: 'Ines Kovač', position: 'Founder, Adriatic Retail Group, Croatia',
      review: 'Their project management is solid.\nThe team is ready to implement the features you actually want, and they say plainly when something is a bad idea.\n\nEvery commitment was met.',
      linkedinurl: '#!', verification: 'G2 Verified',
    },
    {
      photo: { name: 'no-image.svg', url: '' },
      name: 'Anonymous', position: 'Engineering Director',
      review: 'Every optional branch off: no photo, no profile link, no verification badge. The card still has to hold its shape beside three that do.',
      linkedinurl: '', verification: '',
    },
  ];

  const grid = document.querySelector('#story-grid');
  GRID.forEach((item, i) => {
    const slot = document.createElement('div');
    slot.id = `story-grid-${i}`;
    grid.appendChild(slot);
    createApp(Review, { item, className: '' }).mount(slot);
  });

  // Review renders its avatar through LazyImage, so the swap has to go through
  // data-src — see placeholders.js for why setting .src alone loses a race.
  usePlaceholders('#story-rev1 img, #story-grid img', '../assets/images/sb-avatar.svg');
</script>
</DocPage>
